Environmental Impact Assessment (EIA)
Project BAVER - Blast-Activated Viscous Elastic Retardant
Version 2.0 | Public Domain | CC0 1.0 Universal
EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
Project BAVER proposes an aerial-delivered, explosively dispersed fire suppression system using biodegradable components. This assessment evaluates potential environmental impacts across terrestrial, aquatic, atmospheric, and biological domains. The system demonstrates significant net environmental benefits compared to conventional wildfire suppression methods, with all impacts classified as Low to Negligible when deployed according to established protocols. Key findings indicate:

Chemical Components are either USDA Forest Service-approved fire retardants or food-grade, biodegradable polymers with established non-toxicity profiles.

Blast Effects are localized (<500m radius) with temporary disturbance characteristics.

Aquatic Impacts are prevented by mandatory 300m setback protocols.

Soil & Vegetation impacts are neutral to beneficial, with components decomposing within 3-6 months.

Carbon Footprint is 70-80% lower than equivalent water/retardant delivery systems due to reduced aircraft sorties.

Overall Environmental Risk Rating: LOW with proper deployment protocols.

1.0 ASSESSMENT METHODOLOGY
1.1 Scope & Boundaries
Temporal Scope: Immediate (detonation) to 12-month post-deployment

Spatial Scope: 500m radius from detonation point (impact zone)

Comparative Baseline: Current wildfire suppression methods (water, Phos-Chek, foams)

Regulatory Framework: NEPA, CEQA, FIFRA, Clean Water Act compliance

